# Context7 usage

Context7 (`user-context7` MCP) retrieves up-to-date documentation and code examples for any library or framework. Use it when you need current API references, configuration options, or usage patterns.

## Tools

1. `resolve-library-id` — resolve a library name to a Context7-compatible ID (must call first)
2. `query-docs` — query documentation using the resolved library ID

Always call `resolve-library-id` before `query-docs` unless the user provides an ID in `/org/project` format.

## When to use

- Look up current Helm chart values and configuration options
- Verify API signatures, function parameters, or config schema
- Get code examples for a specific library version
- Check default values before overriding them in manifests
- Confirm environment variable names, CLI flags, or config file formats

## When to use DeepWiki instead

Context7 pulls from official documentation. DeepWiki reads source code and repository internals. Pick the right tool:

| Need                                                                | Tool     |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |
| Official docs, API reference, config schema                         | Context7 |
| Source-level behavior, antipattern detection, privacy/security audit | DeepWiki |
| Environment variable names documented in README                     | Context7 |
| Environment variable names buried in source code                    | DeepWiki |

Use both when deploying a new app — Context7 for configuration, DeepWiki for vetting.

## Query tips

- Be specific: "How to configure PostgreSQL connection in Authentik Helm chart" beats "authentik config"
- Include version context when relevant — `resolve-library-id` returns available versions
- Max 3 calls per question per tool — use the best result you have after that
- Pick libraries by name match, source reputation, snippet count, and benchmark score
